WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:07.700
I everybody, I’m Jordan Auten, the producer
and co-director of the short film GRIT and

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.059
I’m here to guide you through the Must Do’s
of producing your own SAG short film.

00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:12.059
1.

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:14.339
Start with the Script.

00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:17.600
Before you get started, you want to have a
really great script.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.120
What story are you telling and what message
are you sending to the world?

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.891
For example with our film GRIT, when I read
the script, I knew it was something I wanted

00:00:25.891 --> 00:00:31.190
to direct and produce because it was really
witty, it was really funny and because it

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:36.800
was an inspiring story about women, choice
and motherhood.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.550
As a filmmaker, those are the types of stories
I want to put in the world, have a story that

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.400
aligns with your vision as a filmmaker.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.400
2.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.030
Production Team.

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:49.700
Next you’ll want to secure your production
team.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.960
You must have a producer who is resourceful
and thorough when researching the rules and

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.720
guidelines within the SAG process.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.400
Ask your filmmaker friends for recommendations
for production people.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.189
Our budget allows a production team with a
producer, co-producer and a production manager.

00:01:06.189 --> 00:01:07.200
3.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.219
Paperwork.

00:01:08.219 --> 00:01:11.229
Next you’ll want to file your SAG paperwork.

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:15.670
In order to produce a SAG project you must
have a qualified signatory who produces the

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:16.670
film.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:21.320
Your producer can be the signatory or you
can create a separate business like an LLC

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.320
or a DBA.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.500
You’ll want to consult a lawyer or a tax
professional as to the pros and cons of each.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:33.560
Keep in mind that whomever ends up being the
SAG signatory will technically own the rights

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.020
to the film once it’s complete.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:41.109
Take the time to learn the difference between
a short film contract and the new media contract

00:01:41.109 --> 00:01:42.929
and decide what’s best for your production.

00:01:42.929 --> 00:01:47.110
(Hint: It will depend on what you choose for
your primary distribution.)

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:48.179
4.

00:01:48.179 --> 00:01:50.340
Workman’s Comp.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.639
Purchase workman’s compensation insurance
for your production.

00:01:54.639 --> 00:01:58.420
SAG requires for your talent to be covered
and it’s very important.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.779
5.

00:01:59.779 --> 00:02:01.149
Payroll.

00:02:01.149 --> 00:02:03.389
Hire a payroll company for your cast.

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:07.880
SAG has specifics you want to follow when
it comes to following all of the union fees

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.099
and ensure everyone’s safety.

00:02:10.099 --> 00:02:11.099
6.

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:12.439
Final Thoughts.

00:02:12.439 --> 00:02:17.349
I highly recommend you get to know the SAG
rules regarding short films and create a professional

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:19.980
working environment for everyone on set.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.599
Have an amazing shoot and happy shooting.

